Section 5 - Policy Statement
(4)
Business decisions at the University need to be made in the face of substantial uncertainty and ambiguity in the dynamic and rapidly changing higher education environment. To prosper in this operating context the University needs to take actions which will expose it to risk. The focus of the University is to ensure a sound understanding of the risks that it faces, so that it can maximise benefits and minimise negative outcomes in the achievement of its objectives. Decisions need to be informed by a comprehensive understanding of acceptable risk taking and where risk must be controlled, transferred or avoided.
(5)
The University’s risk management approach is underpinned by the following guiding principles:
creating a risk-aware culture, where the identification, assessment and management of risk is acknowledged as a driver of positive outcomes;
applying risk management practices as part of strategic, business planning and day-to-day decision making;
facilitating consideration of the balance between risk and benefit in the pursuit of investment initiatives;
informing the effective allocation of resources to control risks that could prevent the achievement of objectives;
assisting the University to operate safely and securely;
implementing a robust governance framework to guide and assess the compliance of risk management activity across the University; and
providing assurance to senior management and Council that strategic and significant operational risks are managed effectively.

Risk, Audit and Insurance Team
(19)
The Risk, Audit and Insurance team works closely with business areas to develop, implement and support effective risk management processes and to promote a culture of risk awareness throughout the University. Their responsibilities include:
developing and enhancing processes, methodologies and a common language to identify, assess and manage strategic and operational risks;
developing, facilitating the implementation and continuous improvement of the University’s Risk Management Framework;
reporting of risk activities to CGRIASC and SEG, which encompasses various tasks including, but not limited to:
quarterly review of the Enterprise Risk Register;
annual review of the Risk Appetite Statement;
annual review of the Assurance Map;
quarterly internal audit updates; and
ensuring the completion of a biannual internal audit plan that adopts a risk-based approach to ensure the effectiveness of management controls;
providing guidance and advice to support risk management activities across the various business areas, promoting continuous improvement and accountability;
collaborating with Portfolios and Divisions across the University to ensure that ORRs are thoroughly reviewed and updated at least twice annually; and
providing risk management training to enhance risk-aware behaviour and ensure consistent application of the Risk Management Framework across the University.
